523</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B7"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gonzalez</surname><given-names>EA</given-names></name><name><surname>Moore</surname><given-names>FA</given-names></name><name><surname>Holcomb</surname><given-names>JB</given-names></name><name><surname>Miller</surname><given-names>CC</given-names></name><name><surname>Kozar</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name><name><surname>Todd</surname><given-names>SR</given-names></name><name><surname>Cocanour</surname><given-names>CS</given-names></name><name><surname>Balldin</surname><given-names>BC</given-names></name><name><surname>McKinley</surname><given-names>BA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Fresh frozen plasma should be given earlier to patients requiring massive transfusion</article-title><source>J Trauma</source><year>2007</year><volume>62</volume><fpage>112</fpage><lpage>119</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17215741</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B8"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Duchesne</surname><given-names>JC</given-names></name><name><surname>Holloway</surname><given-names>VL</given-names></name><name><surname>Weintraub</surname><given-names>SE</given-names></name><etal></etal></person-group><article-title>Review of current blood transfusions strateties in a mature level I trauma center. Were we wrong for the last 60 years? [abstract]</article-title><source>J Trauma</source><year>2007</year><volume>63</volume><fpage>481</fpage></citation></ref><ref id="B9"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Johansson</surname><given-names>PI</given-names></name><name><surname>Stensballe</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Rosenberg</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Hilslov</surname><given-names>TL</given-names></name><name><surname>Jorgensen</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Secher</surname><given-names>NH</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Proactive administration of platelets and plasma for patients with a ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm: evaluating a change in transfusion practice</article-title><source>Transfusion</source><year>2007</year><volume>47</volume><fpage>593</fpage><lpage>598</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17381616</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B10"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gonzalez</surname><given-names>EA</given-names></name><name><surname>Jastrow</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Holcomb</surname><given-names>JB</given-names></name><name><surname>Kao</surname><given-names>LS</given-names></name><name><surname>Moore</surname><given-names>FA</given-names></name><name><surname>Kozar</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Early achievement of a 1:1 ratio of FFP:PRBC reduces moratality in patients receiving massive transfusion [abstract]</article-title><source>J Trauma</source><year>2008</year><volume>64</volume><fpage>247</fpage></citation></ref><ref id="B11"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sperry</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Ochoa</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Gunn</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><etal></etal></person-group><article-title>FFP:PRBC transfusion ratio of 1:1 is associated with significant lower risk of mortality following massive transfusion [abstract]</article-title><source>J Trauma</source><year>2008</year><volume>64</volume><fpage>247</fpage></citation></ref><ref id="B12"><citation citation-type="other"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sperry</surname><given-names>JL</given-names></name><name><surname>Ochoa</surname><given-names>JB</given-names></name><name><surname>Gunn</surname><given-names>SR</given-names></name><name><surname>Alarcon</surname><given-names>LH</given-names></name><name><surname>Minei</surname><given-names>JP</given-names></name><name><surname>Cuschieri</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Rosengart</surname><given-names>MR</given-names></name><name><surname>Maier</surname><given-names>RV</given-names></name><name><surname>Billiar</surname><given-names>TR</given-names></name><name><surname>Peitzman</surname><given-names>AB</given-names></name><name><surname>Moore</surname><given-names>EE</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A FFP:PRBC transfusion ratio &#x0003e; = 1:1.5 is associated with a lower risk of mortality following massive transfusion</article-title><source>J Trauma</source><year>2008</year><comment></comment></citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>